course: everyday
lesson: 401
title: Fighting games at a friend's house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Theo's big brother Callum has Mortal Kombat, a fighting game with a big «18» on the box. One Saturday Theo's parents popped out to the shops, and Theo texted the boys: «callum's out, come round NOW». Ollie, Leo and Kai were on his sofa in ten minutes. Ollie isn't allowed 18 games at home, so his heart was banging before the game even loaded.
explain: It was gorier than any of them expected. Leo announced he had «seen all the fatalities already, they're nothing» — and then watched the whole thing through his fingers, eyes shut. Kai said Tekken was totally fine anyway, because «it's just kicking, no blood». Nobody mentioned that Tekken has its own age rating, and it isn't 7. When Zara from next door knocked to join in, Theo said, «Nah, it's for boys.» Zara rolled her eyes: «I beat you at every racing game you own, Theo.»
explain: Then came the group chat. Theo posted a photo of the screen: «MK at mine lol». Ollie's mum is in that group chat with the other parents, because Theo's mum had added her. By the time Ollie got home she was waiting in the hall. «So,» she said, «how was Mortal Kombat?» The worst part, Ollie said later, wasn't the game. It was that she found out before he told her.
explain: Compare that with Jayden. His dad plays a 16 fighting game with him on Sunday mornings, sitting right next to him. When something nasty happens on screen, they talk about it: «That's a stunt actor's motion capture — in real life that would put you in hospital.» And at Mia's house the rule is «nothing over 12». The boys groaned when they found out — and then had a brilliant afternoon on a racing game and a building game anyway.
explain: Age ratings tell you what is IN a game — violence, blood, scary bits, bad language — not how hard it is. A game is not «for boys» or «for girls»; it's for whatever age the rating says. And every house has its own rules: being a good guest means keeping to the rules of your own parents, even when the adults in charge aren't watching.
fact: Video game age ratings, such as PEGI in the UK and Europe and ESRB in the USA and Canada, show which ages a game's content is suitable for.
fact: Ratings are about content such as violence, blood, fear and bad language, not about how difficult a game is.
fact: Mortal Kombat games are rated 18 by PEGI and M (Mature 17+) by ESRB because of their very gory violence.
fact: A game with no blood can still be rated 12 or 16 for its fighting and violence.
fact: Different families have different rules about games, and a guest's own parents' rules still count.
fact: Things done in secret often come out, through photos, chats or brothers and sisters.
fact: Parents are usually more upset about being lied to than about the thing itself.
fact: Games are not «for boys» or «for girls»; anyone of the right age can play them.
fact: Pretending you are not scared is very common, and many people cover their eyes at gory bits.
fact: Playing a game with a parent who talks about it can make it less frightening and easier to understand.
exercise: Why was Ollie's heart banging before the game even loaded? | He knew he wasn't allowed 18 games | He was excited to win his first round | The sofa was too small | He had run all the way there
exercise: When did Theo invite the boys round? | When his parents were out at the shops | When his brother asked him to come and play too | When his mum said it was fine | When it was his birthday
exercise: What does an age rating tell you about a game? | What content is in it, like violence or fear | How hard it is to win and how many levels it has | How much it costs | How long it takes to finish
exercise: Kai said Tekken is fine because it has no blood. Is he right? | No, games without blood can still be rated for violence | Yes, ratings only count blood, nothing else | Yes, kicking games are always rated 3 because they're cartoons | No, Tekken is full of blood
exercise: What was Leo really doing during the gory bits? | Watching through his fingers with his eyes shut | Taking notes on every move so he could learn | Laughing louder than anyone else in the room | Playing on his phone
exercise: Why do you think Leo pretended he'd seen it all? | He didn't want the others to think he was scared | He really had seen it all before at his cousin's | He wanted to be the best player | Theo told him to say it
exercise: Was Theo right that the game was «for boys»? | No, games are not for boys or girls | Yes, girls aren't allowed fighting games | Yes, the box says so | Only fighting games are for boys
exercise: How did Ollie's mum find out? | Theo posted a photo in a group chat | Ollie told her straight away when he got home | Callum rang her | She guessed it from the look on his face
exercise: What upset Ollie most in the end? | That his mum found out before he told her | That he lost every single round against Theo | That the game was too easy | That Zara came round
exercise: What was different about Jayden playing a 16 game? | His dad played with him and talked about it | He played it alone at night with the sound off | He played it at a friend's in secret | His dad didn't know about it
exercise: What did Jayden's dad explain about the moves on screen? | In real life they would put you in hospital | They are safe enough to try at school if you're careful | Real people get up again straight away | They are easy to copy
exercise: Mia's house rule is «nothing over 12». What is a good guest's response? | Keep to the rule and play something else | Sneak the game on when her parents go out | Tell Mia that her parents are mean and unfair | Go home and refuse to ever come back again
exercise: Ollie is at Theo's and an 18 game goes on. Whose rules should he follow? | His own parents' rules | Theo's big brother's rules | Whatever the others are doing | Nobody's, as no adult is home
exercise: What could Ollie have said when the game went on? | «Not allowed that one, let's play something else» | «Turn it up louder» | «I'll play but don't tell anyone, OK?» | Nothing, and keep it secret forever and ever
exercise: What would have taken real courage for Ollie when he got home? | Telling his mum himself before she found out | Deleting the group chat so nobody saw it | Saying Theo forced him | Hiding in his room all evening
exercise: Why did the boys still have a brilliant afternoon at Mia's? | Lots of games are fun without being 18 | Mia let them play the 18 game anyway, just once | They didn't play any games | They went to Theo's instead
test: «my parents r out, callum's got MK, come round quick». What's the wise move if you're not allowed 18 games? | Say no, or suggest a different game | Run round before the parents get back | Go but don't tell anyone | Go and only watch, that doesn't count
test: «tekken's fine for little kids, there's no blood». True? | No, it's still rated for its fighting violence | Yes, no blood means it's rated 3 | Yes, only blood gets a rating | No, Tekken is rated 18 for all the blood in it
test: «age rating on that one is just how hard it is innit». Right? | It's about what's in the game | Yes, 18 means it's really hard to win | Yes, babies can't do all the buttons | No, it's about how long it takes
test: «leo said he's seen every fatality lol». But he had his eyes shut. What's going on? | He's pretending not to be scared | He has amazing hearing, like a bat | He's seen them all in his bad dreams | He's been practising them every night
test: «girls can't play fighting games, it's for boys». What's the honest answer? | Rubbish, games are for anyone | True, it's a rule printed on the box | True, girls get scared too easily | Only if a boy says they can play
test: «zara's beaten u at every racing game theo». What does this show? | Being good at games has nothing to do with boy or girl | Racing games are for girls, fighting games are for boys | Theo let her win | Zara must cheat
test: «nobody's gonna find out, chill». How likely is that? | Not very, photos and chats spread fast | Totally safe, parents never check | Only if someone films it | Parents can't read group chats
test: «ur mum's in the group chat mate. ur dead». What would make things best with Ollie's mum now? | Telling her the truth himself, without excuses | Saying the photo is fake and made on a computer | Blaming Theo's brother | Saying nothing until she forgets
test: «why's jayden allowed a 16 game and i'm not, so unfair». Best answer? | Each family decides, and his dad plays it with him | Jayden is secretly 16 and just small for his age | His dad doesn't know | Ratings don't apply to Jayden at all
test: Teacher: «Who decides which games you play at a friend's house?» | Your own parents, even when you're away | Whoever owns the console, because it's their house | The oldest child there | Nobody, it's a free house
test: «mia's mum says nothing over 12, what a bore». Is it a boring afternoon then? | No, plenty of great games are 12 and under | Yes, all the good games are 18, the rest are baby games | Yes, so sneak the 18 one on | No, because they'll break the rule
exam: Identify the information conveyed by an age classification affixed to a video game. | The suitability of its content, including violence and fear, for particular ages | The degree of skill required for its completion by an ordinary player | The retail price recommended by the publisher | The duration of play required for its completion
exam: Ascertain whose regulations govern the activities of a minor visiting the residence of a peer in the absence of the peer's parents. | Those established by the minor's own parents | Those determined by the eldest sibling present | Those prevailing in no particular household whatsoever | Those proposed by the owner of the console in question
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fighting game in which no blood is depicted? | It may nevertheless be classified as unsuitable for younger children on account of its violence | It is necessarily suitable for all ages, there being no blood | It is exempt from any age classification | It is classified solely according to its difficulty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a parent who learns of a prohibited activity from a third party. | The parent is frequently more distressed by the concealment than by the activity | The parent is invariably indifferent to the matter | The parent is obliged to remain silent | The concealment diminishes the parent's concern
